GROUNDS FOR FAIL — criterion (e), negative-zero rendering regression at
sites #2/#3 (healthcareTotal/livingTotal, metrics.go:422/455), live-data
reachable and NOT refund-dominant.

Method: cp -a the tree twice ("after" = HEAD/working tree as-is; "before"
= same tree with ONLY the 5 CB7 sites reverted to math.Abs, `go build`
verified clean in both). Wrote a throwaway cmd/cb7check loader mirroring
dataloader.New+storage.New+DataLoader.LoadData (read-only; storage.New
only os.Stat's for a .encrypted marker, confirmed absent) against the
REAL /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2/data (13 CSVs, 1275 txns after
processing — untouched, never opened for write, verified with
`git diff --stat` before/after = unchanged). Computed metrics.Calculate
for: the real default dashboard range (YTD Jan-1→maxDate, mirroring
resolveDateRange in handlers.go exactly), whole-ledger, current calendar
month, and every calendar year/month present (17 ranges).

Result: TotalExpenses/Living/NetSavings/SavingsRate/CombinedCumulativeDelta
matched exactly (byte pattern, not just value) between before/after on
every populated range — the arithmetic itself is correct and CB1's "zero
refund-dominant ranges" premise holds. BUT `healthcare_total` differs
between before (`0`) and after (`-0`) on 17 of 17 populated
calendar years/months in the live ledger (2025 whole year, and every
2025-02 through 2026-05 month present except the few months with actual
HI spend) plus the empty current month. Confirmed with `math.Signbit`:
`math.Abs(healthcareOutflows.SumAmount())` is ALWAYS +0 for a zero input
(Go's math.Abs unconditionally clears the sign bit — verified with a
throwaway probe: `math.Abs(-0.0)` → Signbit=false); the new
`-healthcareOutflows.SumAmount()` (metrics.go:422, one of the 5 CB7-
converted sites) produces IEEE negative zero whenever that category nets
to literally zero for the window — the common case for any month with no
"Health Insurance" transactions.

Consumer impact — traced to `internal/templates/render.go:425 formatMoney`:
`negative := v < 0` is FALSE for -0.0 (IEEE: -0.0 < 0 is false), so the
function does NOT take its negate-and-prefix-"-$"-branch; instead it
falls through with v still -0.0, and `fmt.Sprintf("%.2f", v)` — which DOES
honor the sign bit — emits "-0.00", producing a literal `"$-0.00"` (dollar
sign glued to a negative number) instead of `"$0.00"`. Confirmed with a
standalone reproduction of the exact formatMoney algorithm:
`formatMoney(negZero) == "$-0.00"`, `formatMoney(posZero) == "$0.00"`.

Reachable render sites (both consume `.Metrics.HealthcareTotal`/
`.Metrics.HealthcareActual`, which inherits the sign from site #2 with NO
further correction — `healthcareActual = healthcareTotal / coverageMonths`
preserves negative zero):
- `web/templates/components/kpis.html:105` — "Monthly Healthcare" KPI
  headline, UNCONDITIONAL (no `HasHealthcareTarget` gate on the number
  itself) — renders "$-0.00" whenever `coverageMonths > 0` and that
  window's HI category nets to exactly zero, which is true for nearly
  every month/year in the live ledger per the probe above.
- `web/templates/components/kpis.html:173` — "Health: $X of $Y" line,
  gated on `.BudgetVerdict.Healthcare.Configured` (renders whenever a
  healthcare target is configured in the What-If plan, which this
  household's plan is per prior CB-series work on exactly this feature).

This is a genuine, in-scope regression: math.Abs (immune to signed zero)
is exactly what CB7 replaced at these 5 sites per the spec's own mandated
idiom ("`-set.SumAmount()`... no new helper"); the negative-zero exposure
is a direct, foreseeable consequence of that specific conversion, not a
pre-existing or out-of-scope defect. It violates CB7-RUN-SPEC.md
criterion (e) verbatim: "every current dashboard range... render[s]
BYTE-IDENTICAL before/after... the live ledger has no refund-dominant
range" — these 17 ranges are NOT refund-dominant (they are exactly zero,
not net-positive/credit), so the stated exception does not apply, and the
rendered string differs ("$0.00" → "$-0.00") on live, currently-reachable
data. No committed test exercises a healthcare-window (or living/total)
that nets to exactly zero, so nothing caught it (checker-tests' mutation
sweep only proves the SIGN is load-bearing, not the zero-boundary).

